fact WorkItem(id: "WI-652", description: some(value: "WI-650 follow-up: close the coverage gaps in check_eq_override_backing (the load-time guard that flags eq(map,map) over an unbacked Eq override). It is BEST-EFFORT over operands the typer stamps concretely as the carrier sort in rule/op bodies; a /code-review (high) surfaced these escape routes, all LATENT today (no stdlib/example/test triggers them; suite green) but each a silent-misdecide the guard is meant to catch: (1) COMPOUND operands — operand_unbacked_eq_carrier bails on inferred_type()==None, so eq(put(empty(),1,2), m) / eq(build_map(x), other()) (Apply/Constructor operands, not var leaves) escape; same leaf-reliance check_one_spec_op_requirement has. (2) DOT-FORM a.eq(b) — WI-279/WI-350 dot dispatch rewrites the receiver's eq to the carrier's OWN op Map.eq, whose functor is NOT Eq.eq/Eq.neq, so is_eq_call misses it; need to also recognize a direct call to an unbacked-carrier own eq op. (3) CONSTRAINT/guard bodies — constraints live in kb.guards (Vec<Guard>), NOT self.rules, so live_rule_ids never visits them; a constraint c :- eq(m1,m2) over maps loads clean (also verify guard-body operands even carry stamped inferred_type). (4) POLYMORPHIC abstract-T operands — operation same(a:T,b:T)=eq(a,b) requires Eq[T] instantiated at T=Map reads operand sort as abstract T, never concretizes to Map; inherent to a load-time inferred_type check (same class as the neq under-determination, WI-651). (5) WI-625 INTERACTION — carrier_has_unbacked_eq_override deliberately excludes the is_builtin backing leg (so Eq.eq's builtin-ness doesn't mask the empty Map override); when the WI-625 host bridge registers Map.eq AS a resolver builtin (no body_node, no rule), this predicate will WRONGLY read it unbacked and keep rejecting map compares — WI-625's implementer must update the backing predicate (or the check) then. Also a latent FALSE-POSITIVE: a carrier backing eq via an equational '=' definition (eq(?a,?b)=rhs, UNINDEXED from rules_by_functor per WI-139/WI-627) is invisible to the general-clause probe and would be flagged spuriously — reconcile with op_backed's eq_defined leg (but NOT its plain non-fact-rule leg, which would count Map's get-rewrite law and mask the real bug). ALTITUDE/DEDUP (fold-when-touched, no perf impact measured — WI-650 added ~0.2s to a ~9s debug stdlib load): the pass adds 2-3 extra whole-KB body traversals duplicating check_rule_body_requirements + check_operation_bodies, and check_occ_eq_override_backing is a 4th copy of the stack+for_each_child+Apply/Constructor/Instantiation walk (a shared walk_body_occurrences helper would collapse all four); a single parameterized backed-by(kind) helper should unify carrier_has_unbacked_eq_override with op_backed. Acceptance: pick the high-value gaps (compound operands + dot-form + constraints are the reachable ones) and close them with tests mirroring map_eq_error_test.rs; document any deliberately-left-open (polymorphic-T); full suite green. Follow-up to WI-650 (see also WI-651 neq)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T20:22:29Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-652", author: "user", content: "PERF MEASUREMENT CORRECTION + user-reported regression (2026-07-07): WI-652's own text says 'no perf impact measured -- WI-650 added ~0.2s to a ~9s debug stdlib load'. That is UNDERSTATED. On the anthill-todo CLI (embedded stdlib+bundle, debug), check_eq_override_backing costs ~1.5s: disabling it drops type_check_sorts from 3.63s -> 2.09s (ANTHILL_LOAD_TIMING=1, repo project, 114 sorts). User observed the slowdown directly ('was not a problem yesterday', pre-WI-650 07-06) and pinpointed the cause as 'why load stdlib 4 times?' -- i.e. the 4 whole-KB body walks in type_check_sorts_typed: check_operation_bodies + record_find_dictionary_grounding(WI-300) + check_rule_body_requirements(WI-642) + check_eq_override_backing(WI-650). The DEDUP (shared walk_body_occurrences collapsing all four) is therefore a real perf win (~1.5s recoverable from WI-650's walk alone, more from sharing the other three), NOT just an altitude cleanup. NOTE for the dedup: check_eq_override_backing only fires on user/project code (the stdlib never compares Maps by WI-650's design), so scoping its walk to non-stdlib bodies alone would recover the ~1.5s for CLI consumers. Deferred by user (2026-07-07): document-only, no fix this session.", at: "2026-07-07T10:09:56Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-652", author: "user", content: "PERF-CORRECTION SUPERSEDED (2026-07-07, current main, 117 sorts, debug): the earlier 2026-07-07 note on this item -- that the dedup is a real perf win with ~1.5s recoverable from check_eq_override_backing -- is itself now corrected by direct per-pass measurement (see WI-653 corrected-diagnosis feedback). check_eq_override_backing costs 2ms, not 1.5s; the three cheap post-walks (WI-300 0.5ms + WI-642 30ms + WI-650 2ms) sum to ~33ms, so collapsing them into a shared walk_body_occurrences saves at most ~33ms. The dedup reverts to its ORIGINAL framing: an ALTITUDE / DRY cleanup, NOT a perf win. The real WI-652 content is unchanged and still valuable on correctness grounds: closing the coverage gaps in check_eq_override_backing (compound operands + dot-form a.eq(b) + constraint/guard bodies are the reachable ones), plus the single parameterized backed-by(kind) helper unifying carrier_has_unbacked_eq_override with op_backed. No perf urgency; scope this on correctness/altitude.", at: "2026-07-07T16:14:54Z")
